Presentation
============

We have a database of persons and ask Xapian:

    "the men who are not deleted, or the women who are not deleted,
     born in 2010"

Only one person is born in 2010, and that person is deleted: we expect
nobody. Xapian 2.0.0 and 2.1.0 return the deleted person. Xapian 1.4.30
returns nobody, as expected. It only happens on a large enough database,
which is why the test builds about 46 000 persons.


How to run
==========

    $ python3 test_xapian.py [N]

or in C++:

    $ g++ -O2 -o test_xapian test_xapian.cc $(xapian-config --cxxflags --libs)
    $ ./test_xapian [N]

N is the number of persons (default 46342). Both exit with 1 if the bug is
present, 0 otherwise. To run both against several Xapian install prefixes:

    $ python3 test.py /usr ~/opt/xapian-2.1.0 /usr/local

docker/build.sh builds given Xapian versions from the official sources and
runs the tests in docker (not needed to reproduce).


The test
========

The in-memory database holds N persons:

  - every person has the term sex:male, except person 2;
  - person 2 has the term sex:female and the birthdate 19850312;
  - person 3 has the terms sex:male and deleted, and the birthdate 20100615.

The birthdate is a value in slot 0 (YYYYMMDD). The query:

    ((sex:male AND (ALL AND_NOT deleted)) OR (sex:female AND (ALL AND_NOT deleted)))
        AND VALUE_RANGE(0, 20100101, 20101231)

where ALL is Query::MatchAll. Our application builds such queries: it adds
"AND NOT deleted" to every sub-query.

With BoolWeight, or with the AND NOT moved out of the OR
((sex:male OR sex:female) AND NOT deleted AND born in 2010), the result is
right.


Example output
==============

Xapian 2.0.0 (same with 2.1.0, Python and C++):

    Xapian 2.0.0, 46342 persons
    Query: men not deleted OR women not deleted, born in 2010
    Expected: nobody

    AND NOT in each branch BM25Weight Got: [person 3: deleted, sex:male, born 20100615]  -> WRONG
    AND NOT in each branch BoolWeight Got: nobody
    AND NOT factorized     BM25Weight Got: nobody
    AND NOT factorized     BoolWeight Got: nobody

    BUG: Xapian 2.0.0 returns a deleted person

Xapian 1.4.30:

    Xapian 1.4.30, 46342 persons
    Query: men not deleted OR women not deleted, born in 2010
    Expected: nobody

    AND NOT in each branch BM25Weight Got: nobody
    AND NOT in each branch BoolWeight Got: nobody
    AND NOT factorized     BM25Weight Got: nobody
    AND NOT factorized     BoolWeight Got: nobody

    OK: Xapian 1.4.30 returns nobody


Where is the bug
================

This comes from reading the xapian-core 2.1.0 sources; it was not traced in
a debugger.

matcher/orpostlist.cc, OrPostList::check():

    if (advance_r) {
        if (r->at_end()) {
            PostList* result = l;
            l = NULL;
            pltree->force_recalc();
            valid = true;          // <-- l_valid is lost
            return result;
        }
    }

(the symmetric block for l has the same problem). When l->check() returns
"not valid" (AndNotPostList::check() on person 3, who is deleted), the code
sets l_did = 0 and advance_l = false. If r is then at end, the OR decays to
l and reports valid = true, although l is on a person it excludes. The
parent AndPostList then accepts person 3.

In 1.4.30, OrPostList::check() keeps the validity of the remaining child
(valid = lvalid / rvalid), hence the right result.

Possible fix: keep l_valid / r_valid and use it when decaying to the
remaining child, instead of valid = true.

Why the threshold: ALL (ContiguousAllDocsPostList) does not override
get_docid_range(), so its range stays [1, 2^32 - 1], and the AndPostList
constructor (matcher/andpostlist.h) estimates the size of
AND(sex:male, ALL) as tf * N / (2^32 - 1), with tf = tf(sex:male) = N - 1.
This estimate rounds to 1 instead of 0 once tf * N >= 2^31:

    N = 46341: 46340 * 46341 = 2147441940 < 2^31   -> OK
    N = 46342: 46341 * 46342 = 2147534622 > 2^31   -> BUG

It changes the order of the sub-queries of the AND, and the faulty path
above is taken. It is a rounding, not an integer overflow. We first saw the
bug on a real glass database; glass and in-memory give the same result.
